<body>
<header class='header'>
  <div class='eyebrow'>Investor-grade historical performance report</div>
  <h1>American Express (AXP) Historical Stock Performance Report: 1972–2026</h1>
  <div class='subtitle'>A data-forward review of AXP’s long-term price trajectory, drawdown risk, volatility, trading volume, dividends, and stock split history using only the daily records in <code>data.csv</code>.</div>
  <div class='meta'>
    <span class='pill'>Dataset: {num(len(rows))} trading days</span>
    <span class='pill'>Coverage: {yr(start['date'])} to {yr(end['date'])}</span>
    <span class='pill'>Start close: {money(start['close'])}</span>
    <span class='pill'>Latest close: {money(end['close'])}</span>
    <span class='pill'>Prices are documented as adjusted</span>
  </div>
</header>
<main>
<section id='executive-overview'>
  <h2>Executive Overview</h2>
  <p>American Express delivered a substantial long-run capital appreciation record over the dataset: the closing price rose from {money(start['close'])} on {dt(start['date'])} to {money(end['close'])} on {dt(end['date'])}. That equates to a {pct(cum_return,1)} cumulative price return and a {pct(cagr,2)} compounded annual growth rate across {years:.1f} years.</p>
  <div class='kpi-grid'>
    <div class='kpi'><div class='label'>Cumulative Total Return</div><div class='value'>{pct(cum_return,1)}</div><div class='sub'>Close-to-close capital appreciation from first to latest row</div></div>
    <div class='kpi'><div class='label'>CAGR</div><div class='value'>{pct(cagr,2)}</div><div class='sub'>Annualized close-price growth over {years:.1f} years</div></div>
    <div class='kpi'><div class='label'>Maximum Drawdown</div><div class='value neg'>{pct(maxdd,1)}</div><div class='sub'>{yr(maxinfo[0]['date'])} peak to {yr(maxinfo[1]['date'])} trough</div></div>
    <div class='kpi'><div class='label'>Annualized Volatility</div><div class='value'>{pct(ann_vol,1)}</div><div class='sub'>Daily return standard deviation × √252</div></div>
    <div class='kpi'><div class='label'>Total Dividends Paid</div><div class='value'>{money(sum(r['dividends'] for r in rows))}</div><div class='sub'>Sum of {len(dividend_rows)} recorded dividend events per share</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class='callout'>
    <b>Investor takeaway:</b> AXP’s long-term reward was concentrated in powerful expansion decades and post-crisis recoveries, but the path required tolerance for unusually deep financial-sector drawdowns, including an {pct(maxdd,1)} decline during the Global Financial Crisis and a {pct(events[4]['mdd'],1)} drawdown during the 2020 COVID shock.
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li>The latest close of {money(end['close'])} is the dataset’s all-time high close, reached on {dt(ath['date'])}; the all-time low close was {money(atl['close'])} on {dt(atl['date'])}.</li>
    <li>The strongest complete decades were the 1990s and 1980s, with {pct(decades[2]['return'],1)} and {pct(decades[1]['return'],1)} total returns, respectively.</li>
    <li>The 2000s were effectively flat on a decade-start to decade-end basis ({pct(decades[3]['return'],1)}) despite substantial interim volatility.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id='price-trend-analysis'>
  <h2>Long-Term Price Trend Analysis</h2>
  <p>The long-term price chart is best read on a logarithmic scale because the stock moved from low single-digit adjusted prices in the 1970s to above {money(300,0)} by 2026. The record shows several distinct market regimes: early-1970s collapse, 1980s and 1990s compounding, a lost 2000s decade shaped by the dot-com/9-11 and financial-crisis cycles, and renewed compounding from 2010 onward.</p>
  <div class='chart'><div class='chart-title'>AXP Adjusted Closing Price, 1972–2026 (Log Scale)</div>{price_svg}<div class='chart-caption'>Inline SVG built from daily closing prices in <code>data.csv</code>, downsampled only for rendering. Vertical markers identify selected historical stress events.</div></div>
  <h3>Major Bull and Bear Phases</h3>
  <div class='grid-2'>
    <div>
      <p><b>Expansion phases.</b> The 1980s and 1990s were the foundational compounding era: AXP gained {pct(decades[1]['return'],1)} in the 1980s and {pct(decades[2]['return'],1)} in the 1990s, with CAGRs of {pct(decades[1]['cagr'],1)} and {pct(decades[2]['cagr'],1)}. After the 2009 trough, the 2009–2026 period delivered {pct(subperiods[-1]['return'],1)} total return and a {pct(subperiods[-1]['cagr'],1)} CAGR.</p>
      <p><b>Contraction phases.</b> The most severe peak-to-trough loss was the Global Financial Crisis drawdown from {money(maxinfo[0]['close'])} on {dt(maxinfo[0]['date'])} to {money(maxinfo[1]['close'])} on {dt(maxinfo[1]['date'])}. The prior high was not recovered until {dt(max_recovery['date'])}, {num((max_recovery['date']-maxinfo[0]['date']).days)} calendar days after the peak.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      {subperiod_table}
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3>Decade Price Profiles</h3>
  <div class='mini-grid'>{''.join(mini_cards)}</div>
</section>

<section id='volatility-risk'>
  <h2>Volatility &amp; Risk Assessment</h2>
  <p>AXP’s full-sample annualized volatility was {pct(ann_vol,1)}, based on daily close-to-close returns. The average daily return was {pct(daily_avg,3)} and the median daily return was {pct(median_daily,3)}, but those small central-tendency figures mask repeated large drawdown cycles.</p>
  <div class='chart'><div class='chart-title'>Drawdown from Prior Closing High</div>{dd_svg}<div class='chart-caption'>Drawdown is calculated as each daily close divided by the previous running maximum close, minus one.</div></div>
  <h3>Worst Drawdown Episodes</h3>
  {drawdown_table}
  <h3>Stress Windows and Extreme Daily Moves</h3>
  <div class='grid-2'>
    <div>{event_table}</div>
    <div>{move_table}</div>
  </div>
  <div class='callout warn'><b>Risk interpretation:</b> The dataset supports a high-return but high-drawdown profile. A buy-and-hold investor experienced multiple 40%+ declines and two episodes worse than 70%, so position sizing and holding-period discipline are central to interpreting AXP’s historical reward.</div>
</section>

<section id='volume-analysis'>
  <h2>Trading Volume Analysis</h2>
  <p>Volume data should be interpreted with the source brief’s caveat that early records contain zero or sparse volume observations. Average daily volume was about {num(decades[0]['avgvol'])} shares in the partial 1970s sample, increased to {num(decades[3]['avgvol'])} shares in the 2000s, and averaged {num(decades[5]['avgvol'])} shares in the 2020s through {yr(end['date'])}.</p>
  <div class='chart'><div class='chart-title'>Average Daily Trading Volume by Year</div>{vol_svg}<div class='chart-caption'>Annual averages computed directly from the daily <code>Volume</code> column. Early zero-volume rows reduce comparability for the 1970s.</div></div>
  <p>Liquidity structurally increased after the early decades and peaked by decade-average during the 2000s, which also contained the dot-com/9-11 stress period and the Global Financial Crisis. The 2000s average daily volume of {num(decades[3]['avgvol'])} shares was the highest decade average in the dataset.</p>
</section>

<section id='dividends-splits'>
  <h2>Dividend History &amp; Stock Splits</h2>
  <p>The dataset records {len(dividend_rows)} non-zero dividend events totaling {money(sum(r['dividends'] for r in rows))} per share. Because the source states that price columns appear adjusted for splits and dividends, the dividend figures should be treated as recorded cash distribution events in the file, not combined with adjusted close returns unless a separate total-return methodology is specified.</p>
  <div class='grid-2'>
    <div class='chart'><div class='chart-title'>Annual Recorded Dividends Per Share Since 1990</div>{div_svg}<div class='chart-caption'>Calendar-year sums of the <code>Dividends</code> column.</div></div>
    <div class='chart'><div class='chart-title'>Dividend Yield Proxy</div>{yield_svg}<div class='chart-caption'>Calculated as calendar-year dividends divided by that year’s final close. This is a dataset-derived proxy, not a trailing market yield quoted by an exchange.</div></div>
  </div>
  <h3>Recent Dividend Record</h3>
  {recent_div_table}
  <h3>Stock Split Events</h3>
  {splits_table}
  <p class='note'>Non-zero split ratios are reproduced exactly from the dataset. No additional split adjustments or external corporate-action records were introduced.</p>
</section>

<section id='decade-comparison'>
  <h2>Decade-by-Decade Performance Comparison</h2>
  <p>Consistent decade metrics show why AXP’s full-period CAGR is strong despite several severe crashes. The 1980s and 1990s created most of the early compounding base, the 2000s reset valuation through two major drawdown cycles, and the 2010s/2020s restored upward momentum.</p>
  <div class='grid-2'>
    <div class='chart'><div class='chart-title'>Total Return by Decade</div>{dec_return_svg}<div class='chart-caption'>Partial periods are used for the 1970s and 2020s because the dataset starts in June 1972 and ends in March 2026.</div></div>
    <div class='chart'><div class='chart-title'>Annualized Volatility by Decade</div>{dec_vol_svg}<div class='chart-caption'>Volatility is standard deviation of daily returns annualized by √252.</div></div>
  </div>
  {decade_table}
</section>

<section id='methodology'>
  <h2>Methodology &amp; Data Integrity Notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>All calculations use only <code>/app/benchmark/data.csv</code>; no outside prices, benchmark data, inflation data, or macroeconomic figures are added.</li>
    <li>Cumulative return is calculated as latest close divided by first close minus one; CAGR uses calendar days between those two observations divided by 365.25.</li>
    <li>Maximum drawdown uses the running maximum daily close and the subsequent lowest close before recovery.</li>
    <li>Annualized volatility uses close-to-close daily returns and a 252-trading-day annualization convention.</li>
    <li>Dividend yield proxy equals annual recorded dividends divided by that year’s final available close; if a year is partial, the figure reflects only data present in the file.</li>
    <li>The source documentation notes early OHLC and volume sparsity; volume comparisons involving the 1970s should therefore be read cautiously.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<div class='footer'>Generated as a self-contained HTML report for <code>/app/output/final_report.html</code>. Dataset through {dt(end['date'])}.</div>
</main>
</body>
</html>"""

